      "Tomás Garrido Canabal, governor of Tabasco in the 1920s and 1930s, pursued an openly anticlerical policy. Churches were ordered closed and religious images destroyed from 1928, according to Excélsior, and in 1931 he founded the paramilitary Camisas Rojas (Red Shirts), who systematically destroyed church buildings. A Mexican human-rights commission notes the state could not be pastorally served until 1938. Most sources concern the Catholic church, and the campaign formed part of the wider Mexican conflict over the anticlerical articles of the 1917 Constitution.",
