      "The Central American Mission, founded in Dallas in 1890 by Cyrus Scofield to evangelize the five republics, sends Samuel Purdie, who lands at Acajutla on 12 July 1896. He works in Santa Ana and Metapán while a colleague, Bender, serves San Salvador. Purdie died of tetanus about thirteen months later, but the permanent evangelical work he began is counted as the start of the Salvadoran evangelical church. The Bible colporteur Francisco Penzotti had already sold books in the country in 1893.",
